# converted from 03-three-days.md
@title Three Days
@bg blackhouse
@mood grief

narrate She wanted to go outside.
narrate He said no, and she looked at him with the specific disappointment of a person who has been told no by someone who is usually a pushover, and he said yes, and wrapped her in three blankets and carried her into the garden and sat her in the chair with the terrible cushion and let her watch the light.
narrate She talked for four hours.
narrate He could not remember, afterwards, a single thing she said. That was the bargain — it had taken four years and it was still taking, the way a tide is still going out after the water has stopped looking like it is moving — and by the second week he could not have told anyone what they talked about on the first day. He remembered only the shape of it. He remembered that she laughed.
narrate He remembered, because he wrote it down that night, in the margins of a healer's ledger he kept for the village:
narrate He wrote it three times because he did not trust it once.
narrate The grey was at her elbows.
narrate He saw it at noon, when he changed her dressing, and he stood there with his hands full of linen and looked at it and did not say anything, and she watched him not say anything.
narrate She was seven. She had been ill for eleven days. She had watched him sit at the beds of forty dying people in her short life, because he was the only healer in forty miles and she had come with him to most of them, and she had learned what his face did.
narrate She did not ask.
narrate That was the second day: she did not ask, and he did not tell, and they spent the day pretending together with enormous and mutual effort, and he went outside in the evening and was sick into the herb bed.
narrate She woke before him.
narrate He had fallen asleep in the chair — the chair with the cushion, the terrible cushion, her cushion — and when he woke she was sitting up and looking at him with her head tilted the way she did when she had decided something.
corin neutral Da.
maeve neutral Mm.
corin neutral I want to tell you something and I want you to listen properly.
narrate He sat up. Something in his chest had gone very still.
maeve neutral All right.
corin neutral I've known since the second day,
narrate He did not move.
corin neutral I knew on the first day, actually,
corin neutral But I wasn't sure on the first day. I was sure on the second day. When you changed the dressing and you looked at my arm and then you looked at the wall.
narrate She said it simply. She was seven and she said it the way she said everything, with her head tilted, and she was not accusing him of anything, and that was the worst part, that was the part he would still be failing to survive twelve years later.
maeve neutral You knew.
corin neutral Yes.
maeve neutral And you—
corin neutral I thought if I was all right,
corin neutral you'd be all right. For a bit.
narrate He had asked for three days in which she would be herself and would not be afraid.
narrate He had spent twelve hours of the first day congratulating himself on how well it had worked.
narrate She had spent three days performing being herself, for him, because she could see what he was doing and she had decided — at seven, with eleven days of the Fever in her blood — that her father had paid for something and that she was not going to let it be a waste.
maeve neutral Was it hard?
corin neutral Only a bit.
corin neutral The pretending was hard. The being all right was easy. I am all right, Da. That's the bit you got right.
narrate She made him tell her the story about the hole in the field.
narrate He told it. He told it properly, the way he had told it when she was six — that it was where the Mire kept its secrets, and that if you were very good and very quiet you could hear them, and that the important ones were the ones nobody could say out loud.
narrate She listened with her eyes closed.
maeve neutral Did you go there?
narrate He hesitated.
corin neutral ...Yes.
maeve neutral What did you ask for?
narrate He could not answer.
maeve neutral That's all right,
maeve neutral I know what you asked for. I worked it out on the second day.
narrate She opened her eyes and looked at him, and she was not sad, and she was not frightened, and she looked like a person who has finished a long piece of work and is pleased with it.
corin neutral You asked for me,
corin neutral That's the most anyone's ever done, isn't it.
narrate She died at four in the afternoon.
narrate He was telling her about the river — the time she fell in, when she was four, when she went in sideways and came up furious and told him it was the river's fault — and she was smiling, and she said it was, and then she did not say anything else.
narrate He kept talking for a while. That was instinct, or it was hope, or it was the last thing he had. He finished the story. He told her the ending — that he had carried her home and she had made him promise never to tell anyone she had cried — and then he sat with her in the quiet room, and the fire he had built for her burned down to nothing, and he did not move.
narrate It was Maeve who came.
narrate Two days later, or three — he could not have said. She walked in without knocking, the way she always did, and she stood in the doorway and looked at him sitting in the chair with his daughter's hand in his, and she did not say anything.
narrate She banked the fire.
narrate She opened the shutters.
narrate And then she sat down on the floor beside the chair, because she was sixty and her hip was bad and she did not care, and she said:
maeve neutral You will not survive this.
narrate He did not answer.
maeve neutral You will not survive this,
maeve neutral and you will get up anyway, and those are not contradictory.
narrate He sat there.
maeve neutral I can't put her in the ground,
corin neutral I know.
maeve neutral I can't, Maeve.
maeve neutral I know,
maeve neutral That's why I'm here.
